# Internationalization

All user-visible strings come from the i18n catalogs. Hard-coded user-facing text in
product code is a release-blocking defect.

## Rules

- Strings come from `src/i18n/locales/` via the translation hook (`useTranslation` /
  `t(...)`). No inline English in JSX, alerts, toasts, or error messages shown to users.
- Keys are **namespaced by feature**: `onboarding.welcome.title`, `settings.account.delete`.
- **Never concatenate** translated fragments to build sentences — the locale file owns the
  full sentence; use interpolation variables for dynamic parts.
- Every new key is added to **all supported locales in the same change**. Copying the
  English text into non-English catalogs as a placeholder is forbidden.
- Interpolation variable names, placeholders, and line-break structure must match across
  locales.
- Brand and platform terms ({{PROJECT_NAME}}, `Face ID`, `Touch ID`) stay untranslated when
  identity requires it.
- User-facing error messages are i18n keys or error codes resolved at render time — stores
  and rules modules never emit raw display strings.

## Directional layout (RTL readiness)

- Use logical directional properties (`marginStart`, `paddingEnd`) instead of hard-coded
  `left`/`right` on directional components.
- Validate list and chat layouts with long content and RTL where supported.

## Verification

- Run the locale-consistency check (key parity across catalogs) whenever locale files are
  touched; wire it as an npm script (e.g. `npm run i18n-verify`) and a CI gate.
